    <h1>Exercise Name: Seated Palms-Up Plate Wrist Curl</h1>

    <u><b><h4>Overview Exercise</h4></b></u>
    The exercise shown in this video is the Seated Palms-Up Plate Wrist Curl (using weight plates instead of a barbell or dumbbells).

This is an isolation exercise designed to target the inner underside of your forearms using weight plates for grip challenge.

 Target Muscles: Forearm Flexors (Flexor Carpi Radialis, Flexor Carpi Ulnaris)—highlighted in orange/red on the inner forearms in the animation.
 Secondary Focus: Pinch grip strength, as holding smooth weight plates actively engages your fingers and thumb pressure.

</div></div>

 <h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1> 

<u><b><h4>1. Starting Position:</h4></b></u>
 Kneel or sit alongside a flat bench.
 Rest your forearms flat across the bench, allowing your wrists to hang comfortably over the edge.
 Hold a weight plate in each hand with an underhand grip (palms facing upward).


<u><b><h4>2. The Execution (Curl Up):</h4></b></u>
 Exhale and curl your wrists upward toward the ceiling as far as possible without lifting your elbows or forearms off the bench.
 Pause briefly at the top to squeeze the flexor muscles.


<u><b><h4>3. The Lowering Phase:</h4></b></u>
 Inhale and slowly lower your wrists back down to full extension in a smooth, controlled motion.





<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Targeted Inner Forearm Thickness:</b> Isolates the muscle belly of the flexors to add mass and definition to the inside of your arms.<br>

 <b>◉Pinch & Finger Strength:</b> Using weight plates instead of handles forces your fingers to squeeze harder, improving overall hand endurance.<br>

 <b>◉Wrist Health & Stability:</b> Strengthens the connective tissue in the wrist joint, protecting against injuries in heavy pulling movements like deadlifts, rows, and pull-ups.



<h1>4. Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

As displayed on screen in the video:

| Parameter | Recommended Target |
| --- | --- |
| Sets | 3 Sets |
| Repetitions | 12 Reps |
| Rest Period | 45–60 seconds between sets |

> Pro Tip: Select a plate weight that allows you to complete 12 clean reps without jerking your elbows off the bench. Quality of motion matters more than heavy weight for forearm building.
